    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d562">

                <pb facs="0669" n="659" sameAs="frus:frus1942China/pg659"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M)/7163: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Gauss</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-07-07T20:00:00-04:00">July 7, 1942—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>603. Your no. 691, June 11, 10 a.m. Please report as closely as possible amounts
                    of mercury on hand ready for export, specifying <pb facs="0670" n="660"
                        xml:id="pg660"/> any quantity that may be ear-marked for Soviet Russia, as
                    well as quantity free mercury available for the United States. Where is this
                    mercury located and is it sufficiently near Kunming to be picked up immediately
                    provided air space is available, as indicated in Kunming’s no. 38, July 2, 12
                        noon?<anchor n="25" xml:id="d562-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Is the Department to understand that, in addition to any quantities on hand, this
                    year’s production will be some 200 tons of which 180 will be committed to Russia
                    for loan payments?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
